# {{project_name}} — Execution protocol ({{initiative_title}})

> **What this is.** The operating manual of the build. It defines HOW the catalog in
> `detailed-plan.md` gets executed: **wave sessions** (not 1 task = 1 session),
> self-verified loops with the **DoD-auto as the exit criterion**, workflow fan-out
> where parallelism helps, and **mandatory adversarial verification at ⚠gates**. The
> copy-paste prompts per wave are at the end (§7). Keep in sync with `task.md`
> (state) and `master-plan.md` (rationale). Prompts are tuned for Fable at high
> effort but run on any model.

## 1. Principles

1. **Session = one wave: 1–4 coupled tasks, one branch/MR per wave.**
2. **The loop ends by DoD, not by fatigue.** Each wave defines its **DoD-auto**: a
   list 100% verifiable by command. While it isn't green the loop continues:
   implement → verify → fix. Max 5 rounds; if the SAME failure doesn't converge in
   **3 attempts** → stop, report, **never degrade tests to pass**.
3. **DoD-auto ≠ DoD-human.** Actions only {{owner}} can take (dashboards, DNS,
   purchases, approvals) live in the **DoD-human**: when the DoD-auto is exhausted
   the task is marked `blocked` with the human checklist rendered at close. It only
   becomes `completed` when {{owner}} confirms.
4. **Implementers never self-approve at gates.** On ⚠gate waves the loop does NOT
   close without **adversarial verification**: a sub-agent that did NOT see the
   implementation, given only the diff + the DoD + that wave's attack checklist (§6).
5. **Inviolable decisions** (no session may contradict them — from
   `master-plan.md` §8): {{list_them_inline}}.
6. **No hardcoded dates**: the logbook uses the date of the day of execution.

## 2. Canonical wave-prompt template

```text
Start a clean session in EXECUTION mode (auto-accept edits — e.g. `claude
--permission-mode acceptEdits`). Do NOT enter plan mode: the plan IS this prompt
(# SCOPE); the only re-planning trigger is a diverging census.
Load the skill `{{project_context_skill}}`, read
`{{docs_dir}}/execute.md` (this protocol).

# GOAL — Wave <Wx>: <titles>
Work EXACTLY the tasks <IDs> (specs: detailed-plan.md — read them in full; they are
the specification).
DoD-auto (the loop's EXIT criterion; everything by command):
- [ ] <command> → <expected result>
- [ ] (if the wave touches UI) a REVIEWED screenshot of the render — see "VISUAL CHECK"

# TASKS
Create one task per ID (TaskCreate), mark it in_progress on entry and completed on
verification. Checkpoint commit (Conventional) at each internal task close.

# SCOPE (manifest)
<per task, inlined from its Scope manifest card in detailed-plan.md: Modify/Create
with a 1-line why per file · Read first exemplars · Impact (shared surface ← N
consumers; contract kept or adaptation in scope; DoD covers the consumers) · Census
command → expected N · tolerance (exact/±1 for small counts; ±X% only for large) ·
Symbol notes when they fit the budget, else the pointer to the card. The
investigation already ran: do NOT re-derive these lists. Census FIRST; divergence
beyond tolerance ⇒ STOP and re-scope against detailed-plan. Budget ~15 lines/task
(hard cap 25 for multi-file tasks), ≤60/wave — overflow stays in the card.>

# LOOP
implement → verify (run the DoD-auto) → fix → repeat. Max 5 rounds; 3 attempts on the
same failure without converging → stop and report (don't degrade tests, don't mark
done what isn't done).

# VISUAL CHECK (MANDATORY if the wave touches UI: .pug/.html/.scss/.css/components)
grep + build only prove the code is THERE, not that it LOOKS right (they never catch real width,
wrong-language text, overflow/overlap). Before marking a UI task completed: render the page in headless
Chrome and review the screenshot YOURSELF — local server (full env) or the deployed URL. Use a screenshot
helper (Playwright/Puppeteer + the system browser); capture desktop (≥1280) AND mobile (375), both languages
if i18n. Verify width/responsive (not "compacted"), i18n, no overlaps, design-system fonts/colors.

# WORKFLOW (fan-out)
<wave-specific parallelization: sub-agents with EXCLUSIVE ownership of disjoint
files/folders; on ⚠gates: adversarial verification with the attack checklist from §6>

# GUARDRAILS & CLOSE
Touch nothing outside the tasks' scope. No secrets in code/commits. When the DoD-auto
is green: <delivery per §3>, then run the FULL doc-sync at close (§2b — "append the
logbook" means ALL of it), and list any pending DoD-human marking those tasks
blocked.
```

## 2b. Doc-sync at close (mandatory — "append the logbook" means ALL of this)

Closing a wave (DoD-auto green) — and every later status change (e.g. {{owner}}
merges a ⚠gate) — updates ALL plan docs in ONE pass, one Conventional commit:

1. **`task.md`** — logbook entry with the extended schema (date of execution ·
   branch/MR · tasks closed · **files touched** · **manifest drift** · notes · wall
   clock) AND the wave-map status flip.
2. **`execute.md` §7** — this wave's prompt header flip (`☐` → `✅ DONE (date)` /
   `🔄` / `⛔`).
3. **`detailed-plan.md`** — mark the tasks done; update their Scope manifests with
   what was ACTUALLY touched; for every LATER wave whose manifest names surfaces this
   wave changed, refresh its file lists and census expected counts.
4. **`execute.md` §7** — regenerate the prompt of any later wave whose manifest
   changed in (3): its `# SCOPE` must match detailed-plan.
5. **Delivery message — ENDS with a links block, no filler**: the last paragraph of
   the close message to {{owner}} is a compact links block, in this order: (a) the
   wave's viewable result when it renders (site/page via the LAN-IP URL so it opens
   from a phone — `127.0.0.1` does not count; start the preview server yourself if
   none runs, with HTTP Range support or video is dead on iOS; a served PDF; a
   report); (b) ALWAYS the project's canonical artifacts from
   `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json` (plans, prompts, mockups…) — even when this wave
   rendered nothing new; (c) repo paths and commit hashes, one line each —
   reference, not delivery. The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close when the
   block is missing.
6. **Project context skill — DELTA refresh**: with the logbook's files-touched list,
   update only the affected references of `{{project_context_skill}}` (usage-map
   consumer counts, folder inventories, entry hints if new surfaces were born) and
   bump their `last_scanned`. Use the generator's incremental mode
   (`scan_project.py --update <paths>`) when available. Every closed wave leaves the
   brain AND the code map fresh — freshness never depends on manual re-scans.
7. **{{master-plan.md execution-state block / plans.html / prompts.html}} — regenerate
   AND REPUBLISH**: flip the row/badge in the file, then, in the SAME session,
   republish every HTML registered in `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json` that changed on
   disk to its SAME URL (Artifact tool with `url`; read the live version first — the
   publish requires it). A badge flipped on disk without a republish leaves the
   shared link stale (lesson learned: a wave closed with the published plan still
   showing "pending"). New artifact ⇒ new row in `artifacts.json` in the same
   session. The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close while a registered
   artifact is stale.
8. {{If this plan family coexists with an INCUMBENT protocol (the project already
   keeps its own live TASKS/EXECUTION docs): state explicitly which docs THIS family
   owns and which belong to the incumbent — never write to the incumbent's docs from
   these prompts; report the pending incumbent update to {{owner}} instead.}}
9. **COMMIT AND PUSH the docs repo — the doc-sync does not exist until pushed.**
   `{{docs_dir}}` is its own NESTED git repo inside the container; the harness /
   environment header reports the working directory as "not a git repo" — that is the
   CONTAINER, don't believe it: verify with `git -C {{docs_dir}} status`. Close with
   `git -C {{docs_dir}} add -A && git commit -m "docs({{wave}}): …" && git push`. An
   unpushed logbook is invisible to the next session and to {{owner}} from another
   machine (lesson learned: a whole wave's docs once sat uncommitted for days).

⚠gate nuance: at session exit the wave is "verified, pending {{owner}}", not ✅;
re-run this doc-sync when {{owner}} pushes/merges.

